usb: gadget: f_acm: don't wait for host connect in acm_stdio_start()

acm_stdio_start() looped on `while (!acm_connected(dev))`, calling
ctrlc() on every iteration. ctrlc() polls tstc() on the CURRENT
stdin -- and stdin is already pointed at "usbacm" at this point,
since acm_stdio_start() runs from inside console_assign()/iomux_doenv()
while the device is being assigned. So ctrlc() re-enters
acm_stdio_tstc() -> dm_usb_gadget_handle_interrupts(f_acm->udc),
nested on top of the outer loop's own acm_connected() ->
dm_usb_gadget_handle_interrupts(f_acm->udc) call on the same UDC.
That nested/re-entrant interrupt servicing corrupts ci_udc's
queue-head/endpoint state (observed as "CACHE: Misaligned operation
at range [00000001, ...]" and a data abort a moment later).

DFU's equivalent loop in common/dfu.c (run_usb_dnl_gadget()) also
calls ctrlc(), but never hits this: its stdin stays on plain serial
throughout, so its ctrlc() never touches the gadget it just
registered. That's the actual difference between DFU (works) and
ACM (crashed) -- not a missing init call, both use the identical
udc_device_get_by_index() / g_dnl_clear_detach() / g_dnl_register()
sequence.

There's no need to wait here at all. acm_stdio_puts()/putc() already
push into tx_buf before checking f_acm->connected and simply return
without transmitting when not connected -- output is buffered and
flushed by the next __acm_tx() once a host attaches. acm_stdio_tstc()
returns 0 on an empty rx_buf with a single (non-reentrant) interrupt
poll. So returning immediately after g_dnl_register() is sufficient:
the console comes up right away, works normally once/if a host
attaches, and degrades to "output silently buffered" rather than
hanging or crashing when it doesn't.

--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/f_acm.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/f_acm.c
@@ -646,12 +646,12 @@
 	else
 		return -ENODEV;
 
-	while (!acm_connected(dev)) {
-		if (ctrlc())
-			return -ECANCELED;
-
-		schedule();
-	}
+	/*
+	 * Do not wait for a host here: ctrlc() would re-enter this same
+	 * UDC's interrupt handling while stdin is mid-assignment to this
+	 * device, corrupting ci_udc state. putc()/puts()/tstc() already
+	 * tolerate no host being attached yet.
+	 */
 
 	return 0;
 }
